<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="expectations">
  <title>Expectations For Assignments</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      This section outlines the basic expectations for the different
      types of assignments in the class. I will describe these expectations
      to the <em>highest standard</em>, and provide some advice on what to do
      when you don't think you're quite there.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-engagement">
    <title>Engagement Credits</title>
    <p>
      Every assignment in this category is grade for <alert>completion and effort.</alert> Stick around
      for most of class and work with another student on the group activities. Ask a question in office
      hours, or be part of a discussion.
    </p>
    <p>
      For reading quizzes and self-reflections, don't just write single line answers. Briefly
      explain your reasoning for reading questions, and be explicit about what you need to improve
      on self-reflections (e.g <q>I'm having trouble visualizing variable mass problems so I'm going
        to (<ellipsis/>)</q>). I'm just looking for your honest effort here.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-homework">
    <title>Homework</title>
    <p>
      Homework is graded on <em>completion</em> and <em>effort.</em> The goal of having you turn
      in homework is so you can get feedback on your work from the course graders. The expectations
      outlined here are a combination of good problem-solving strategy (i.e, what experts do when they
      solve problems) and presentation tips. The presentation part is so your work is readable by
      the grader so you can get accurate and actionable feedback.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Draw clear (large, if you can) pictures which sketch the basic features of the problem and
            make sure that relevant variables are defined. Yes, the <m>x</m> coordinate is usually
            horizontal and positive to the right; <m>T</m> usually means tension and is oriented along
            a rope. We also don't always use the standard axes, so if you are using a different
            convention the grader will have to read your mind. In my experience, not having a diagram
            is the source of <em>most</em> errors that are difficult to track down (e.g <q>oh, this
            tension here is not the same as the tension there!</q>), so you will help yourself
            by having pictures. Make them <alert>as complete as you can.</alert>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Explain your reasoning in words: someone might have told you that equations speak for
            themselves. I regret to inform you that they were not being truthful. Chains of equations
            without explanation of where they came from are not easy to read, and the grader has to
            guess what you were thinking.
          </p>
          <p>
            You don't have to write an essay before every equation, but a brief explanation of where
            the equation is coming from and why you can use it is key for the grader to interpret
            your reasoning and give you good feedback. Please include this. Doing this will likely
            also help you: often, saying something out loud or writing it down might help you
            find a mistake before you commit to it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you can't explain a step, this indicates that there's something to learn from it. If
            you tag a piece of the homework with a star or question mark or some other symbol, this
            alerts the grader to give you qualitative feedback on that particular part of a problem.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Sanity Checks: I won't force you to sanity check every algebraic expression of every problem,
            but the graders should see that you tried to do this for at least a few of the problems. If
            you sanity check a solution and find an error, that's a sign for you to fix it! If you can't
            find the fix, then you can use your sanity check to ask for more feedback.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Evaluating completeness: A complete solution is self-contained, meaning that
            a reader who has the background of a student in the course should be able to
            follow the solution completely from start to finish without doing any extra
            work.
          </p>
          <p>
            Issues with completeness usually arise from gaps in the solution or reasoning.
            Ask yourself: If I got this solution to me as lecture notes, would I have to
            do a lot of extra work to understand it?
            If the answer is <q>yes</q> then you should try to add more detail to your
            solution.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Getting stuck: if you genuinely get stuck on a problem and cannot complete it before
            the deadline, do not turn it in blank. Use the principles above to do as much as you
            are able of the problem and try to identify what is it that is getting you stuck. For
            instance, maybe you realize you don't know what are all the forces that are acting
            on an object. Now you've identified a problem and the graders can be more helpful. Or
            maybe you're having trouble writing a particular condition mathematically. The more
            information you give the graders, the more they can help.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If a significant fraction of your work is incomplete or contains significant errors, 
            you will be asked to meet with an instructor/teaching assistant to discuss your homework
            before you earn credit for it.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-challenge-expec">
    <title>Challenge Problems</title>
    <p>
      The most important expectation of challenge problems is that <alert>individual work
      will not be accepted.</alert> Collaboration is an important part of science and
      engineering, and working in a team requires practice. This is also an explicit
      learning goal for the course.
    </p>
    <p>
      I will have an online sign-in sheet available on Canvas so you can see which students
      do not have partners. I recommend creating a class-wide chat using a platform like
      GroupMe or Teams (yes, this was done in the past) so you don't have to message each
      other using Canvas, which can be difficult. The best way to find a partner is to check
      in with people right before or right after class. I recommend that you find a partner
      ahead of time. You are responsible for finding a partner and arranging time to work
      on challenge problems ahead of time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Problem-solving wise, a first challenge problem attempt will be successful if it is 
      complete, clearly communicated, and also correct with any relevant sanity checks. 
      Think of the challenges as the equivalent of an essay or research paper. If your first 
      submission doesn't meet these criteria, you will get an opportunity to revise it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The revised submission to the challenge problem is expected to be complete, clearly
      communicated, and correct. That said, these problems are hard. On a case-by-case basis,
      your group may earn a mark for a challenge problem if it is clear that the revised version
      has clearly incorporated the feedback from the grader and improved significantly from the
      first version. We will count significant growth as having engaged productively with the 
      challenge problems, so please keep that in mind if they make you nervous.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-exam-expec">
    <title>Fundamental Skills</title>
    <p>
      Exams will follow the same completeness requirements as the homework, but your solutions should 
      now clearly demonstrate that you have mastered the concepts and processes associated with a 
      fundamental skill. Note that this goes beyond simply having right or wrong answers. Sometimes a 
      wrong answer to a problem might be wrong for reasons unrelated to the core ideas of the problem, 
      and your grasp of the core concepts is still evident. 
    </p>
    <p>
      On the flip side, a solution might have a right answer but how you got that right answer does 
      not show evidence of mastery of the core concepts. What this means that we will accept one or 
      maybe two minor errors in your solution, but several minor errors, or any error that calls into 
      question your understanding of the concepts, will lead to a grade of <q>Retry</q> on a problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      A quick note on mathematics and understanding. Physics is a quantitative science, so being
      able to go from conceptual understanding to algebraic representation and solution is what we
      mean by mastering a skill. If you can only do math or only explain things conceptually, then
      something is missing. Some problems may require you to obtain numerical solutions with units. In
      that case, obtaining a numerical value and properly checking your units is considered a fundamental
      part of the problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have any questions about this, please reach out. Do note that we are not looking for
      perfection, since we understand that exams are time limited and that it is hard to be 100%
      right about something when there is time pressure. What we are trying to gauge from your solutions
      is that you can interpret a problem conceptually, represent the concepts mathematically, perform
      the necessary algebra, and evaluate your solution (this last part is a <q>time permitting</q>
      thing, but you should always try).
    </p>
    <p>
      A note on exam strategy: exam 1 problems you show up on exam 2, and exam 2 problems will show up
      on exam 3, etc. If you have to retry 2 exam 1 skills on exam 2, that would mean 7 problems to
      complete in 3h. That's not reasonable and you are not expected to do that. Before the exam,
      think about what your strengths are. On the day of the test, take a quick scan through the problems
      to see which ones you feel the best about. If you shoot to do 5 problems per exam, this will give you
      20 chances to try the 15 learning targets.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>